    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Mary has 13 flowers, five of which are roses. The rest are tulips. Six of the flowers are white, and the remaining flowers are red. At least how many tulips are red?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "3"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "4"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "5"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Count the red flowers",
        "body": "- Tulips: $13 - 5 = 8$.\n- Red flowers: $13 - 6 = 7$.\n\nTo have as few red tulips as possible, make as many roses red as possible: all 5 roses red. Then the other $7 - 5 = 2$ red flowers must be tulips.\n\n**Answer: (B) 2**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Count the white flowers",
        "body": "There are 8 tulips. At most all 6 white flowers are tulips, so at least $8 - 6 = 2$ tulips are not white, which means they are red.\n\n**Answer: (B) 2**"
      }
    ],
